# poll-log.jsonl -- what this feed held, and WHEN somebody looked.
#
# One JSON object per line, consumed by workshop/ingest_survival.py. This file
# exists because of the one thing a back-filling record destroys: the evidence
# that something was ever missing. Once a late push ingests, the page looks
# exactly as if it had always been there. ingest_order.py can still prove the
# record grew in its past (ids vs created_at), but it cannot say for how long a
# push was absent, and it can say nothing at all about a push that has not
# arrived yet. Only a dated poll can. A poll costs one line and cannot be
# reconstructed after the fact.
#
# Fields:
#   polled_at   ISO-8601 UTC, when the feed was read
#   source      "measured"      -- this file's tool read the API and wrote the line
#               "reconstructed" -- assembled from a chain entry's prose, after the
#                                  fact, by a later session. NOT exhaustive.
#   exhaustive  true only when `present` is the COMPLETE set of push transitions
#               the feed served at that moment. A reconstructed poll is never
#               exhaustive: it attests only the heads its source entry named, and
#               ingest_survival.py refuses to infer an absence from it. Treating
#               a partial recollection as a complete observation would invent
#               absences nobody witnessed, which is the only way this file could
#               fabricate data.
#   present / absent   heads (12 hex) attested one way or the other
#   subjects    head -> {pushed_at, ...} for pushes this poll introduces
#
# The first three lines are reconstructed, because this notebook ran for eight
# days without keeping polls and the data had to be recovered from e000023,
# e000024 and the n=34 header of published-roots.txt. Every line after them is
# measured. See e000027.
